{% set homeFaqs = [
  {
    "q": "Why does a circuit breaker keep tripping repeatedly?",
    "a": "A circuit breaker trips to protect your wiring from overheating. Common causes include an overloaded branch circuit drawing excess amperage, a dead short circuit inside an outlet or appliance, a ground fault, or a worn internal trip mechanism that can no longer hold rated load."
  },
  {
    "q": "Is a buzzing, hot, or burning-smelling breaker box an emergency?",
    "a": "Yes. Buzzing, sizzling, warmth on the breaker face, or a burnt plastic odor indicates loose lug torque, oxidized bus stabs, or electrical arcing inside the load center. Turn off the affected breaker if safe, clear the area if sparks or smoke appear, and call a licensed electrician immediately."
  },
  {
    "q": "Can a single faulty circuit breaker be replaced without replacing the whole panel?",
    "a": "Yes—when the main bus bars show no heat pitting or arcing damage and a UL-listed OEM breaker is still available for your panel brand (such as Square D, Eaton, Siemens, or GE), an electrician can test the circuit and swap the defective breaker cleanly."
  },
  {
    "q": "When should a home upgrade from a 100-amp to a 200-amp electrical panel?",
    "a": "A 200A heavy-up is typically recommended when your existing 100A panel has no open slots, shows double-tapped breakers, experiences dimming lights when HVAC or appliances start, uses recalled Federal Pacific (FPE) or Zinsco equipment, or needs capacity for an EV charger or heat pump."
  },
  {
    "q": "Do your participating electricians follow US National Electrical Code (NEC) standards?",
    "a": "Yes. Independent electricians in our US network follow applicable National Electrical Code (NEC) rules and state/municipal inspection requirements, including AFCI/GFCI protection, conductor ampacity sizing, and proper neutral/ground isolation."
  }
] %}

